    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_89c1e8cd44b231183d1e0f8c97e2a79ac277ea31b2db599df3f9e0322aa10157",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Pole data become coefficient-sum scales only through an inversion theorem with adequate analytic control. The one-variable theorem extracts a simple-pole contribution and recovers a partial-sum asymptotic; the cubic quadratic-family construction introduces a discriminant variable, correction polynomials linking two summation orders, and a Perron contour whose poles yield primary and secondary powers.",
